chive.org///web/20160212004033/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U4409-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U4545","title":"CONTEMPORARY DIPLOMACY","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course examines the process of diplomacy; the patterns, purposes, and people that shape the contemporary interactions of states. In the first, entitled \"Making War and Peace\"- we look at a series of the most important episodes in twentieth-century diplomacy. In the second section under the heading \"Professional Norms and Pathologies\"-we consider some of the problems faced by diplomats in any period. The concluding section of the course called \"The Newest 'New Diplomacy'\"- takes up distinctive aspects of diplomacy in the current period: how the United States and other governments have dealt with the proliferation of multilateral organizations (and of weapons of mass destruction), with ethnic warfare and genocide, with the pressures and opportunities of globalization, and with the war on terrorism that began after September 11, 2001.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"John Hirsch","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"64700","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U4545-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004042/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U4545-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6004","title":"PROGRAMMING FOR ENTREPRENEURS","subtitle":null,"desc":"In this course, you will learn the fundamentals of programming so you can start writing web applications that can potentially be used in non-profit or public sectors. The course will be very hands-on and you are expected to code during the class. The topics will include - fundamentals of computer science, programming basics, data structures, client-server architecture, javascript, application programming interface, LAMP stack and web frameworks, design tools, scalability issues and infrastructure for application deployment. We will discuss some of these topics in the context of agile development methodology for startups. If you are interested in building a startup as a social entrepreneur, the tools and methods you learn in this course should help you in coding the first prototype of your application. As part of the final project, you are expected to build a fully functional web applicat","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Sameer R Maskey","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[5,6,7],"start":600,"end":1020,"startLabel":"10:00am","endLabel":"5:00pm","location":"404 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"22948","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6004-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"WORKSHOP","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004044/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6004-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6016","title":"COST-BENEFIT ANALYSIS","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U4200 or SIPA U6400 or SIPA U6401 \nThis course aims to provide an introduction to cost-benefit analysis and the economic evaluation of government or development programs, projects and policies. The course consists of two parts: theory/methodology in the first half of the semester and application of the learned concepts through an analysis of various case studies in the second half. Case studies will cover the full range of possible applications of CBA -from early education, social policy, health, urban planning, transportation and energy to environmental regulations. Case studies will cover both the US and developing country contexts. In the second half of the semester students will be expected to apply what they have learned by carrying out a cost-benefit analysis on a topic of their choice. The project is expected to include all components of a professional CBA - de","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[["SIPA U4200","SIPA U6400","SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"11286","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6016-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004046/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6016-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6016","title":"COST-BENEFIT ANALYSIS","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U4200 or SIPA U6400 or SIPA U6401 \nThis course aims to provide an introduction to cost-benefit analysis and the economic evaluation of government or development programs, projects and policies. The course consists of two parts: theory/methodology in the first half of the semester and application of the learned concepts through an analysis of various case studies in the second half. Case studies will cover the full range of possible applications of CBA -from early education, social policy, health, urban planning, transportation and energy to environmental regulations. Case studies will cover both the US and developing country contexts. In the second half of the semester students will be expected to apply what they have learned by carrying out a cost-benefit analysis on a topic of their choice. The project is expected to include all components of a professional CBA - de","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Eva Weissman","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"411 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["SIPA U4200","SIPA U6400","SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"73329","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6016-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004045/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6016-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6017","title":"INTERNATIONAL TRADE","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6400 or SIPA U6401 \nThe course has two dimensions: theory and policy. In the former, the fundamental models of international trade theory will be presented. Using these models we will try to understand why countries specialize and trade, what determines the pattern of trade (i.e., which country will export which good), and how trade affects relative prices, welfare, and income distribution within a country. The second part of the course deals with issues concerning trade policy.  We will compare the effects of and rationale behind the usage of various policy instruments such as tariffs, subsidies, quotas, etc. The political economy of trade policy and trade policy in developing countries will also be covered. Additional topics may be included at a later stage if time permits.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Benjamin R Mandel","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"413 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["SIPA U6400","SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"61532","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6017-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004047/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6017-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6017","title":"INTERNATIONAL TRADE","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6400 or SIPA U6401 \nThe course has two dimensions: theory and policy. In the former, the fundamental models of international trade theory will be presented. Using these models we will try to understand why countries specialize and trade, what determines the pattern of trade (i.e., which country will export which good), and how trade affects relative prices, welfare, and income distribution within a country. The second part of the course deals with issues concerning trade policy.  We will compare the effects of and rationale behind the usage of various policy instruments such as tariffs, subsidies, quotas, etc. The political economy of trade policy and trade policy in developing countries will also be covered. Additional topics may be included at a later stage if time permits.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[5],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[["SIPA U6400","SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"66899","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6017-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004048/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6017-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6018","title":"INTL FINANCE & MONETARY THEORY","subtitle":"INTRNTNL FINANCE-MONETARY THRY","desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6401 \nThis is a \"methods\" course meant to provide students with the analytic tools necessary to think through \"real life\" international economic policy situations. The class is primarily meant for those interested in working at international financial institutions, the foreign-service, Wall Street, or the financial press. Lectures will, in part, be fairly rigorous though, if the student has taken first year economics, knows basic algebra, and (most importantly) can navigate graphs, he/she will be able to handle the material fairly easily. While theory will at times dominate, its policy relevance will be illustrated through i) l0-minute discussions at the beginning of every class on topical issues; ii) continuous references to recent economic/market episodes meant to illustrate the theoretical material; iii) reading short pieces of Wall Street research that cover time","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[["SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"72800","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6018-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004050/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6018-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6018","title":"INTL FINANCE & MONETARY THEORY","subtitle":"INTRNTNL FINANCE-MONETARY THRY","desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6401 \nThis is a \"methods\" course meant to provide students with the analytic tools necessary to think through \"real life\" international economic policy situations. The class is primarily meant for those interested in working at international financial institutions, the foreign-service, Wall Street, or the financial press. Lectures will, in part, be fairly rigorous though, if the student has taken first year economics, knows basic algebra, and (most importantly) can navigate graphs, he/she will be able to handle the material fairly easily. While theory will at times dominate, its policy relevance will be illustrated through i) l0-minute discussions at the beginning of every class on topical issues; ii) continuous references to recent economic/market episodes meant to illustrate the theoretical material; iii) reading short pieces of Wall Street research that cover time","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Daniel J Waldman","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"76399","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6018-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004049/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6018-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6020","title":"POLITICAL ECON OF EURO AREA CRISIS","subtitle":"POLITICAL ECON OF EURO AREA CR","desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6401 \nThis course will explore the politics and economics of the euro area crisis. We will start by evaluating the economic costs and benefits of a common currency. A brief historical survey will provide insights as to the political and economic interests underpinning European monetary integration. We will then explore the causes of the euro area crisis and analyze the political economy of crisis management. Crisis management and subsequent institutional reforms will be evaluated in terms of both economic rationality and competing political interests. Country case studies will help us understand the economic logic of macroeconomic adjustment as well as the various bail-ins/ bail-outs. We will also evaluate the success/ failure of economic reform in the so-called periphery. Last but not least, we will explore how and why broader institutional reforms (e.g. bail-out re","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Markus H Jaeger","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"409 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[["SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"93750","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6020-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004051/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6020-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6021","title":"EUROPEAN BANKING POST CRISIS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course examines the root causes, implications,  regulatory reforms and prognosis for European Union and non EU countries financial sectors following the financial and currency crisis in 2010 which initially implicated Greece, but  evolved into a larger European banking and market crisis. The course will focus on three main areas: EU and Eurozone political and economic environment; policies and politics: regulatory reforms; country specific analysis of major and minor institutions.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Irene E Finel-Honigman","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"324 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"86396","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6021-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004052/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6021-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6039","title":"INTERNATIONAL BANKING","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6401 & SIPA U6200 \nAn overview of current issues and major trends in global banking, exploring the distinction between developed and emerging markets, and focusing on the perspectives from the different actors and constituencies in the international markets: customers, regulators, governments, rating agencies, institutional investors, multilateral agencies, and management.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Fernando Sotelino","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"410 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["SIPA U6200"],["SIPA U6401"]],"callNumber":"96701","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6039-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004053/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6039-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6041","title":"CORPORATIONS & HUMAN RIGHTS","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Joanne R Bauer","culpa":1,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"402 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"80783","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6041-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004054/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6041-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6042","title":"ENERGY BUSINESS & ECON DEVPT","subtitle":null,"desc":"Energy is a key input and a key business in economic development. The course first develops the current understanding of the economic development process, with a focus on the role of energy, and energy businesses and markets. Then we examine development problems and policies in resource dependent economies, middle income reforming economies, low income economies and conclude with a look at the interface between economic development and environmental protection.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"324 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"17696","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6042-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004055/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6042-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6043","title":"FOUNDATIONS OF SDEV PRACTIC","subtitle":"FOUNDATIONS OF SDEV PRACTICE","desc":"This course aims to provide students with a general introduction to the basic core competencies and practical skills required of a generalist development practitioner.  The course will be offered at a number of universities around the world, and each week students will have the opportunity to learn from an expert practitioner.  Course topics will be grounded in a practical, multi-disciplinary approach that will focus on the inter-relationship of each of the following core fields of study: Public Health, Agriculture and Nutrition, Environment and Climate Science, Technology and Engineering, Economics, Policy, Anthropology and Social Studies, and Management. Both conceptual and practical management issues will be stressed throughout each course topic. The course will incorporate state-of-the-art web-based technologies for sharing lectures across countries, and to facilitate international d","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Shiv Someshwar","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":480,"end":650,"startLabel":"8:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"C03 School of Social Work","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"25945","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6043-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004056/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6043-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6043","title":"FOUNDATIONS OF SDEV PRACTIC","subtitle":"FOUNDATIONS OF SDEV PRACTICE","desc":"This course aims to provide students with a general introduction to the basic core competencies and practical skills required of a generalist development practitioner.  The course will be offered at a number of universities around the world, and each week students will have the opportunity to learn from an expert practitioner.  Course topics will be grounded in a practical, multi-disciplinary approach that will focus on the inter-relationship of each of the following core fields of study: Public Health, Agriculture and Nutrition, Environment and Climate Science, Technology and Engineering, Economics, Policy, Anthropology and Social Studies, and Management. Both conceptual and practical management issues will be stressed throughout each course topic. The course will incorporate state-of-the-art web-based technologies for sharing lectures across countries, and to facilitate international d","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"404 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"27548","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6043-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004057/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6043-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6045","title":"INTERNATIONAL CAPITAL MARKETS","subtitle":null,"desc":"The course will acquaint you modern international capital markets. You can expect to learn a substantial amount of up-to-date detail and some useful theory. Specifically, we will survey global markets for credit, equity, foreign exchange, foreign exchange derivatives, futures, interest rate swaps, credit default swaps and asset backed securities. In each case, we will learn the highlights of payments and settlement, documentation, regulation, applications for end-users, related economic theory and pricing models. The class will cover options and asset pricing theory; however, the treatment will be informal and designed to help develop intuition. One lecture each will be devoted to international banking (with an emphasis on changing capital regulation), investment banks, and hedge funds.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[5],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"60536","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6045-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004059/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6045-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6045","title":"INTERNATIONAL CAPITAL MARKETS","subtitle":null,"desc":"The course will acquaint you modern international capital markets. You can expect to learn a substantial amount of up-to-date detail and some useful theory. Specifically, we will survey global markets for credit, equity, foreign exchange, foreign exchange derivatives, futures, interest rate swaps, credit default swaps and asset backed securities. In each case, we will learn the highlights of payments and settlement, documentation, regulation, applications for end-users, related economic theory and pricing models. The class will cover options and asset pricing theory; however, the treatment will be informal and designed to help develop intuition. One lecture each will be devoted to international banking (with an emphasis on changing capital regulation), investment banks, and hedge funds.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Richard Robb","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"78032","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6045-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004058/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6045-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6046","title":"GLOBAL MEDIA:INNOVATION&ECON;","subtitle":null,"desc":"The world of media continues to change quickly. Legacy media outlets are struggling with their role in this new world and working to make more use of technology. Media starts ups find it hard to build audience, get sustained attention and establish credibility. Everyone worries about how they will be able to fund newsgathering and what future business models will look like. This course will explore some of the possibilities for innovation and discuss how new practices can help address some of the difficulties faced by journalists. We will consider what it takes to create new sites or tools that can actually find funding and we will meet different journalists and experts involved in some of the projects springing up around the world. By the end of this course, students will understand the basics of some media theory and scholarship and be able to speak knowledgably about the journalism cl","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Anya Maria C Schiffrin","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"64032","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6046-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004100/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6046-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6053","title":"CREATING A SOCIAL ENTREPRISE","subtitle":"CREATING A SOCIAL ENTERPRISE","desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Sarah M Holloway","culpa":1,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"324 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"76597","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6053-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004101/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6053-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6061","title":"GLOBAL ENERGY POLICY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Brief review of energy balance fundamentals. World energy outlook. Key uncertainties. Resource revolution. Energy efficiency, the hidden fuel. Electricity systems. Architecture of electricity markets. Thermal generation. Renewable energies. Learning curves. LCOE´s. Oil market: demand and supply, reserves, conventional and unconventional oil, energy from America, prices. Natural gas market: demand and supply, reserves, differences relative to oil markets. Resources to reserves: oil and natural gas. The renewable energies opportunity. Earth energy balance. Economics of climate change. Emissions scenarios. International climate negotiations. China´s energy challenge.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Manuel Pinho","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_Pinho","days":[4],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"11003","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6061-20153-002","openTo":["Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","School of Professional Studies"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004103/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6061-20153-002/"},{"code":"INAF U6061","title":"GLOBAL ENERGY POLICY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Brief review of energy balance fundamentals. World energy outlook. Key uncertainties. Resource revolution. Energy efficiency, the hidden fuel. Electricity systems. Architecture of electricity markets. Thermal generation. Renewable energies. Learning curves. LCOE´s. Oil market: demand and supply, reserves, conventional and unconventional oil, energy from America, prices. Natural gas market: demand and supply, reserves, differences relative to oil markets. Resources to reserves: oil and natural gas. The renewable energies opportunity. Earth energy balance. Economics of climate change. Emissions scenarios. International climate negotiations. China´s energy challenge.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Manuel Pinho","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_Pinho","days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"418 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"60831","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6061-20153-003","openTo":["Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","School of Professional Studies"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004104/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6061-20153-003/"},{"code":"INAF U6061","title":"GLOBAL ENERGY POLICY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Brief review of energy balance fundamentals. World energy outlook. Key uncertainties. Resource revolution. Energy efficiency, the hidden fuel. Electricity systems. Architecture of electricity markets. Thermal generation. Renewable energies. Learning curves. LCOE´s. Oil market: demand and supply, reserves, conventional and unconventional oil, energy from America, prices. Natural gas market: demand and supply, reserves, differences relative to oil markets. Resources to reserves: oil and natural gas. The renewable energies opportunity. Earth energy balance. Economics of climate change. Emissions scenarios. International climate negotiations. China´s energy challenge.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Manuel Pinho","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel_Pinho","days":[4],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"88041","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6061-20153-001","openTo":["Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","School of Professional Studies"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004102/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6061-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6065","title":"THE ECONOMICS OF ENERGY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Will we run out of oil? What determines the cost of a ton of coal? Should we subsidize low-carbon or tax fossil energy? Are renewables worth the price tag? This course addresses some of the fundamental questions in energy economics. It covers markets for coal, oil, natural gas and renewables. We will gain an understanding of how the various markets work, how they do not, and what the appropriate regulatory responses are.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Gernot Wagner","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gernot_Wagner","days":[1],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"85800","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6065-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004105/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6065-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6065","title":"THE ECONOMICS OF ENERGY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Will we run out of oil? What determines the cost of a ton of coal? Should we subsidize low-carbon or tax fossil energy? Are renewables worth the price tag? This course addresses some of the fundamental questions in energy economics. It covers markets for coal, oil, natural gas and renewables. We will gain an understanding of how the various markets work, how they do not, and what the appropriate regulatory responses are.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"407 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"86900","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6065-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004106/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6065-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6068","title":"ECON ANALYSIS-ENVIR POLICIES","subtitle":null,"desc":"This is a semester-long introductory course in environmental economics. It is designed to introduce students to economic approaches to understanding and managing pollution and natural resources. There is a wide conception that the environmental and economic systems are fundamentally at odds, but hopefully, by the end of this class, you will have a more refined view. We will start the class by a quick review of the fundamental welfare theorem of economics, which states that under certain conditions, markets outcomes are efficient. This forms the basis for why economists so strongly believe in markets. We will then examine why some of those \"certain conditions\" might not be met for environmental problems, and whether hence government intervention is warranted or whether the market can self-regulate these problems. This forms the basis for the rest of the class where we look in more detail ","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Christian Gollier","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"18496","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6068-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004107/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6068-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6071","title":"ENVIRONMENTAL FUNDAMENTALS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course will explore economic and political models of policymaking; interest-group politics; the connections among expertise, knowledge, and policymaking; and the particular politics of policy issues that cross jurisdictions such as federalism and globalization.  Many of the decisions we make and actions we take have profound environmental effects, yet we often fail to assess the systems of values underlying such actions. The class will introduce these issues in weekly case studies, where the economic and political concept will be highlighted for the case of a particular medium: fishery, forests, oil, water, etc) and also explore the limitations to such organizing principles.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Sara Tjossem","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"405 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"72099","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6071-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004108/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6071-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6071","title":"ENVIRONMENTAL FUNDAMENTALS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course will explore economic and political models of policymaking; interest-group politics; the connections among expertise, knowledge, and policymaking; and the particular politics of policy issues that cross jurisdictions such as federalism and globalization.  Many of the decisions we make and actions we take have profound environmental effects, yet we often fail to assess the systems of values underlying such actions. The class will introduce these issues in weekly case studies, where the economic and political concept will be highlighted for the case of a particular medium: fishery, forests, oil, water, etc) and also explore the limitations to such organizing principles.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"501 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"88785","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6071-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004109/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6071-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6072","title":"ENERGY SYSTEMS FUNDAMENTALS","subtitle":null,"desc":"The purpose of this course is to establish a core energy skill set for SIPA students and prepare them for more advanced energy courses by providing a basic language and toolset for understanding energy issues. Existing energy sources and the infrastructures that deliver them to users around the world are undergoing a period of rapid change. Limits to growth, rapidly fluctuating raw material prices, and the emergence of new technology options all contribute to heightened risk and opportunity in the energy sector.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[5],"start":850,"end":1020,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"5:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"91897","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6072-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004111/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6072-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6072","title":"ENERGY SYSTEMS FUNDAMENTALS","subtitle":null,"desc":"The purpose of this course is to establish a core energy skill set for SIPA students and prepare them for more advanced energy courses by providing a basic language and toolset for understanding energy issues. Existing energy sources and the infrastructures that deliver them to users around the world are undergoing a period of rapid change. Limits to growth, rapidly fluctuating raw material prices, and the emergence of new technology options all contribute to heightened risk and opportunity in the energy sector.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Travis Bradford","culpa":1,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Travis_Bradford","days":[3],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"403 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"96053","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6072-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004110/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6072-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6085","title":"ECONOMIC DEVPT IN LATIN AMER","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Jose A Ocampo","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Antonio_Ocampo","days":[1],"start":660,"end":830,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"1:50pm","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"82532","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6085-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004112/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6085-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6090","title":"TAX POLICY: ECONOMICS & LAW","subtitle":null,"desc":"The class will introduce students to the current research in tax policy (broadly defined) and will give them an opportunity to develop skills in reading and evaluating contemporary economic and legal research related to tax policy. Following the discussion of the fundamental tax policy questions during the first several weeks of the term, the format will shift to a series of weekly paper presentations by leading scholars from around the country, both economists and lawyers. The second part of the course (six weeks) will meet together with the parallel class in the law school.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Wojciech Kopczuk","culpa":5,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wojciech_Kopczuk","days":[2],"start":980,"end":1090,"startLabel":"4:20pm","endLabel":"6:10pm","location":"304 Jerome L. Greene Hall (Law)","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"86549","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6090-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004113/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6090-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6095","title":"FINANCIAL REGULATION","subtitle":null,"desc":"In this course we will study financial market failures due to externalities, incomplete information and financial fragility and we will investigate the ways that a benevolent government might use regulation to improve matters. We will also consider theories about the behavior of regulators drawn from political and behavioral economics. We will examine the difficulties associated with designing financial regulation. We will analyze how regulatory failures contributed to the recent financial crises and discuss post-crises attempts at regulatory reform.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"405 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"96500","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6095-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004114/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6095-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6120","title":"TOOLS/CRAFT-MULTI-PLATFORM","subtitle":null,"desc":"The web opens up exciting possibilities for interaction and new ways to tell stories. We'll introduce students to the world of multimedia storytelling and how it can be applied to organizations working in International Affairs and Development.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Christopher Booker","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"501 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"82300","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6120-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","School of the Arts","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004115/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6120-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6132","title":"AIMING FOR SOCIAL IMPACT","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"71596","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6132-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004116/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6132-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6138","title":"INVESTMENT IN SOCIAL CHANGE","subtitle":null,"desc":"The concept of social impact has gained global attention, though it remains limited in scale within the capital markets. The potential exists to better integrate market forces with broader public outcomes, but new thinking is needed. To effectively contribute to the global dialogue on impact investment, students must be interdisciplinary in their approach - part entrepreneur, part social advocate, and part policy specialist, all within a keen grasp of the dynamics of innovation. This course is intended to accelerate the evolution of Investment in Social Change by looking at the ways these disciplines must work together to craft social innovation. Students will apply formal reasoning to an inherently subjective field, and should leave the course better prepared to (a) balance the dual mandate of return and social change within their own endeavors; (b) understand the economic principles of","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Christopher M Reim","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"405 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"75517","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6138-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004117/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6138-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6163","title":"POLITICAL ECON OF AFRICAN DEVP","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Akbar Noman","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"501B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"67195","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6163-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004118/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6163-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6172","title":"POLITICAL DEVELOPMENT IN DEVELOPING WORL","subtitle":"POLITICAL DEVELOPMENT IN DEVE","desc":"Prerequisites: Restricted to EPD students \nThis course will examine enduring and new debates in political development and their implications for policy and policymaking. Matters of state-society relations, democracy and democratization, how political change occurs, and the role of institutions have been heavily researched and discussed in academia. Those discussions and issues have also shaped policy and policy debates (and not always in a good way) in development, diplomacy and domestic policymaking. The course will examine many of the traditional issues of political development, such as state-society relations, structure versus agency, democracy, as well as new topics including the growth of identity politics and the implications of the rise of the Global South on politics and democracy.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Christopher Sabatini","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"410 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"62601","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6172-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004119/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6172-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6173","title":"MIGRATION & HUMAN DEVELOPMENT","subtitle":null,"desc":"International migration's substantial economic and social effects are at the forefront of today's academic discussion, international debate as well as national policy strategies. This course introduces students to the key notions, norms, and narratives of international migration from economic, legal, sociological, international relations, and normative perspectives. Students will learn about transnational livelihood strategies and channels through which migration and migrants can enhance human development especially in their countries of origin, while creating better opportunities for themselves and contributing to their communities of destination. This includes in-depth discussions of the determinants, flows and effects of emigration, immigration, return, financial and social remittances, and diaspora investment. Highlighting migration phenomena in different scenarios in the global Nort","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Daniel Naujoks","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"405 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"67898","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6173-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004120/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6173-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6186","title":"COMMUNITY ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Andrew Ditton","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"501B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"73250","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6186-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004121/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6186-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6190","title":"COMPL EMERG:ROOT CAUSES-REBLDG","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course forms an introduction to the broader program on humanitarian affairs. We will address the root causes of complex humanitarian emergencies, the practices of humanitarian intervention, the main actors, and the opportunities and dilemmas for rebuilding. We will also discuss the main critiques of humanitarian action and possible alternatives. The course advocates the principle that humanitarian aid should be provided from a (long-term) development perspective? otherwise it can reinforce conflict and exclusion.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Dirk Salomons","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"82449","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6190-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004122/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6190-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6192","title":"SEL ISSUES FED RESERVE & FINANCL MARKETS","subtitle":"SEL ISSUES FED RESERVE & FIN M","desc":"The seminar will offer an insider perspective on the recent activities of the Federal Reserve and the current challenges it faces. It will also provide detailed background on the role and objectives of the central bank and the specifics of its traditional monetary policy and liquidity tools. Additionally, the course will examine certain key concepts about financial institutions and markets, particularly as they relate to financial stability and the Federal Reserve's operating framework. For the Fall 2015 semester, the course will highlight the following topical issues: • Non-bank financial intermediation and the systemic risks that contributed to the financial crisis • The Federal Reserve's crisis actions and subsequent changes to its authority • Regulatory efforts towards strengthening the financial system • The recent evolution and future of the Federal Reserve's monetary policy framew","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Patrick O Dwyer","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"409 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1,"pointsLabel":"1","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"75950","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6192-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004123/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6192-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6207","title":"INTERNATIONAL REPORTING","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Larry Heinzerling","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"402 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"87498","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6207-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004124/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6207-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6209","title":"E-GOVT & DIGITAL DIPLOMACY","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Alexis Wichowski","culpa":1,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"73251","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6209-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004125/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6209-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6211","title":"TECH SOLUTIONS FOR DEVLP&SOC; CHANGE","subtitle":"TECH SOLUTIONS FOR DEVLP&SOC; C","desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Eric D Cantor","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Cantor","days":[1],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"402 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"83748","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6211-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004126/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6211-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6236","title":"HIST OF AMER ECOLOGY & ENVIRON","subtitle":null,"desc":"We will explore various conceptions of nature and ecology in changing ideas of conservation, preservation, the Dust Bowl, the atomic age, growing environmentalism, and the current focus on biodiversity as one route to a sustainable society. We will look at how scientific information has been constructed and used in environmental debates over pollution and overpopulation and will question the utility of distinguishing between \"first nature\" (untouched by humans) and \"second nature\" (nature modified by humans). Along the way, we will address connections between environmentalism and nationalism, the relationship between environmental change and social inequality, the rise of modern environmental politics, and different visions for the future of nature.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Sara Tjossem","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"402 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"93349","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6236-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004127/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6236-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6285","title":"METHOD-DEFENSE ANALYSIS/ASSESS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course is intended to provide students with the tools and knowledge required to assess modern military forces. It covers strategic nuclear forces, as well as conventional air, sea, and land forces. It addresses the technical capabilities of modern military forces, the command, control, communication (C3) and logistics infrastructure that support them, and some of the organizational/political factors that can affect force employment. It will also provide an overview of some defense analysis applications of commercial software such as Google Earth.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Austin G Long","culpa":1,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"1302 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"11206","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6285-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","School of the Arts","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004128/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6285-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6301","title":"CORPORATE FINANCE","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6200 or PEPM U6223 or EMPA U6010 \nCorporate finance is an introductory finance course; it is a core course for students taking the International Finance and Policy (IFP) concentration. The course is designed to cover those areas of business finance which are important for all managers, whether they specialize in finance or not.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Ailsa Roell","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1,3],"start":555,"end":645,"startLabel":"9:15am","endLabel":"10:45am","location":"404 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["SIPA U6200","PEPM U6223","EMPA U6010"]],"callNumber":"23397","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6301-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004129/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6301-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6301","title":"CORPORATE FINANCE","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6200 or PEPM U6223 or EMPA U6010 \nCorporate finance is an introductory finance course; it is a core course for students taking the International Finance and Policy (IFP) concentration. The course is designed to cover those areas of business finance which are important for all managers, whether they specialize in finance or not.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"411 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[["SIPA U6200","PEPM U6223","EMPA U6010"]],"callNumber":"25787","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6301-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004130/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6301-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6346","title":"US ROLE IN WORLD AFFAIRS I","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: Restricted to International Fellows \nThis course will explore the international role of the United States by examining its evolution over time the interests and concepts that underlie it, the domestic debates that have shaped it, the historical turning points that periodically re-shaped it, and some of its most notable successes and failures","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Stephen R Sestanovich","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Sestanovich","days":[2],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"93348","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6346-20153-001","openTo":["Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Public Health","School of the Arts"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004131/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6346-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6359","title":"GLOBAL ECONOMIC GOVERNANCE","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course aims at familiarizing students with major issues surrounding global economic governance and its effects on developing countries. It will start with two general lectures that will deal with the objectives of international cooperation, the historical evolution of the current governance and typologies of the different rules, organization and governance structures that have been created at varied times. It will then deal in detail with major topics in the broad agenda of global economic governance, exploring both issues that are the subject of current debates as well as the institutional questions involved. \"Global economic governance\" is understood in a broad sense, to refer both to global and regional frameworks, as well as those rules of international transactions that have been left to bilateral agreements or are under the domain of national sovereignty. \"Economic\" is also un","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"407 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"60035","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6359-20153-001","openTo":["Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004132/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6359-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6366","title":"GENDER,INEQUALITY & CLASS","subtitle":"GENDER, INEQUALITY & CLASS","desc":"In the last half-century there have been revolutionary changes in the social structures that all previous human societies took for granted. They follow from women's greatly increased access to education and the labor market, but go well beyond this to encompass all of social life. In emerging economics, change is happening far faster than it did in the industrialized West. And one result is that different groups of women lead increasingly distinct lives. Historically, it made good sense to think of women as a ‘sisterhood' with common concerns and interests. This is less and less the case. Different groups of women follow increasingly distinct paths in terms of work, family and marriage patterns, and self-identity. Making enormous strides in the workplace are young, educated, full-time professionals who have put children on hold, and who work in occupations that are highly integrated in g","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Alison Wolf","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"324 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"62207","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6366-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004133/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6366-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6368","title":"WOMEN AND GLOBALIZATION","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course will relate selected aspects of globalization to women's labor force participation and teach students how to design policy under this agenda. With a case-study approach, we will explore how globalization has either fostered or inhibited the utilization of the female talent pool in certain contexts and brainstorm policy interventions from the perspective of a number of different entities (e.g. public/private sector, grass roots, etc.). Case studies will include some of the following geographies: the Middle East, India, Russia, Africa, China, Japan, and Latin America. We will wrap up the practicum by comparing the status of women around the world with that of women in the United States, focusing on lessons learned from a policy perspective. During each class we will invite outside experts in selected fields to both help students define a specific a problem statement and design ","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"501A International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"62301","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6368-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004134/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6368-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6372","title":"POLICY & WOMEN'S LEADERSHIP","subtitle":"POLICY & WOMENT'S LEADERSHIP","desc":"Despite gains in recent years, gender disparities in leadership roles – particularly in the corporate and government sectors – remain significant.  This 7-week course will explore policies within organizations, as well as governmental policies, designed to address gender disparities in leadership roles, examining questions such as: What are the goals such policies are/should be seeking to achieve? What are the best approaches – e.g. gender-focused vs. more broadly crafted policies?  Which approaches are/are not working? What are the unintended consequences of policies designed for this purpose? How do we consider debates in popular culture (from Sandberg to Slaughter) in the context of organizational and governmental policymaking and use them to inform policymaking? What are the limitations on what policy can achieve? The course will begin by briefly exploring historical and current gend","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Michelle Greene","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"73347","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6372-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004135/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6372-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6374","title":"MAINSTREAMING GEN-GLOBAL AFFRS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course introduces students to gender mainstreaming, gender analysis and intersectionality as theory and method, as well as the associated set of strategies, tools and skills applicable to international and public policy contexts. Through a combination of empirical research, structural theorizing, social critique, and case studies, students will become acquainted with the global dimensions of feminist organizing and policy-making necessary for working in a variety of specialty policy fields such as education, public health, international finance, sustainable development, peace and security, organizational management and economic development.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Kristy Kelly","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"418 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"88051","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6374-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004136/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6374-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6383","title":"DYNAMICS OF CYBER CONFLICT","subtitle":null,"desc":"This class examines the dynamics of cyber conflict. We will focus less on the technology of cyberspace than the national security threats, challenges, and policy responses including lessons from history and other kinds of conflict. After taking this course, you will understand about the Internet and Internet-based attacks; how cyber conflicts unfold at the tactical and strategic levels; how cyber conflicts and cyber power are different or similar to conflict and power in other domains; the evolution of US cyber policies and organizations; as well as legal issues and the policies and organizations of other nations. The centerpiece of the course is an exercise to reinforce the fundamentals of national security response to a major cyber incident. Accordingly, you will demonstrate the ability to formulate policy recommendations in the face of the uncertainties of an unfolding cyber conflict.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Jason Healey","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jason_Healey","days":[4],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"14702","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6383-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004137/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6383-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6388","title":"MODERN URBAN TERRORISM","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Mitchell D Silber","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitchell_D._Silber","days":[1],"start":1090,"end":1200,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"8:00pm","location":"1302 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"98452","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6388-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004138/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6388-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6391","title":"CONFLICT RESOLUTION","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course introduces the study and practice of conflict resolution, offering students a broad conceptual framework for more specific strands of study offered by CICR.  It also aims to show how ideas about conflict resolution can cast light on individual conflicts and peace initiatives.  The majority of classes focus on thematic issues and debates, but these are interspersed with classes concentrating on individual conflict situations, to allow students to link theory and practice. Students will be tested on both their grasp of the main themes of the course and their application to specific situations.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Richard Gowan","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"410 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"85783","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6391-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004139/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6391-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6393","title":"EVOLING MILIT STRAT POST 9/11","subtitle":null,"desc":"The World at Night: America's Evolving Military Strategy in an Asymmetrical Age. Drawing from the NASA composite photograph depicting where the world is, and is not, brightly lit at night, the seminar will explore how dynamic demographies, economies, technologies, ideologies, and requirements for natural resources are shaping a minor revolution in military thinking. Students will consider global trends and linkages to better understand the renewed importance of contextual understanding of regional populations, geography, religion and history as they relate directly to accomplishing military objectives in support of national policy.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Eric Olson","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_N._Olson","days":[1],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"801 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"12782","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6393-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004140/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6393-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6405","title":"HUMAN RIGHTS & DEVPT POLICY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Human rights can provide a framework for shaping development policies. How will the observance of human rights criteria in planning, implementing and evaluating development projects and policies contribute to their effectiveness and sustainability? The class will examine development policy choices and their impact by juxtaposing the interests and points-of-view of the various stakeholders involved in designing and implementing development policies.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"63015","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6405-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004141/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6405-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6429","title":"ENERGY INDUSTRY IN THE BRICS","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Adam Shrier","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"501B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"13449","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6429-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004142/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6429-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6475","title":"SOCIAL POLICY & INCLUSIVE DEVPT","subtitle":"SOCIAL POLCY & INCLUSIVE DEVPT","desc":"This course will explore the comparative and global political economy of contemporary systems of social and labor protection in developing (including post-socialist) countries, known as \"welfare regimes.\" Economic globalization, struggles over democratization, shifting and competing policy paradigms at the international level, and the assistance strategies of international financial institution and non-governmental donors will all be discussed as important contextual, and at times decisive, influences.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Scott B Martin","culpa":4,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"60284","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6475-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004143/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6475-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6604","title":"APPLIED ECONOMETRICS","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: SIPA U6501 \nThe goal of this course is to enable students to evaluate the policy relevance of academic research. While academic research frequently considers treatments that approximate a potential public policy, such prima facie relevance alone does not inform policy. In particular, public policy is predicated on the credible estimation of causal treatment effects. For example, although researchers frequently document the strong correlation between years of schooling and better health, this tells us surprisingly little (and arguably nothing) about the health effects of public tuition assistance, compulsory school laws, or any other program that raises educational attainment. Policies guided by statistical correlations - even the regression-adjusted estimates that dominate the academic literature - will frequently have unintended and even perverse real-world effects. Polic","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Douglas V Almond","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"823 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["SIPA U6501"]],"callNumber":"11786","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6604-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004144/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6604-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6643","title":"POLITICL ECONOMY OF SOUTH ASIA","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"S. Akbar Zaidi","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S._Akbar_Zaidi","days":[2],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"402 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"72298","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6643-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004145/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6643-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6680","title":"GEOPOLITICS OF OIL & GAS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course is designed to provide students with a broad understanding of the relationship between U.S. national security and the economics, history, and politics of the international oil and gas industry. It places the current U.S. oil and gas situation in historical context and it analyzes how energy policy affects U.S. relations with other states. The course also examines how other states use their oil and gas resources (or lack thereof) to cooperate and/or compete with the United States. The course focuses primarily on: 1) How the United States obtains its oil and gas supply; 2) the factors driving this acquisition activity, and 3) the foreign policy and global security consequences of this activity.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Jonathan A Chanis","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"324 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"78197","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6680-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004146/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6680-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6680","title":"GEOPOLITICS OF OIL & GAS","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course is designed to provide students with a broad understanding of the relationship between U.S. national security and the economics, history, and politics of the international oil and gas industry. It places the current U.S. oil and gas situation in historical context and it analyzes how energy policy affects U.S. relations with other states. The course also examines how other states use their oil and gas resources (or lack thereof) to cooperate and/or compete with the United States. The course focuses primarily on: 1) How the United States obtains its oil and gas supply; 2) the factors driving this acquisition activity, and 3) the foreign policy and global security consequences of this activity.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Jonathan A Chanis","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"418 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"85801","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6680-20153-002","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004147/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6680-20153-002/"},{"code":"INAF U6735","title":"ISSUES IN RURAL DEVELOPMENT","subtitle":null,"desc":"This is a survey course; students will be exposed to a range of resource persons, ideas and concepts. The objectives of the course are to: improve the understanding of the role and importance of rural development in today's world; develop awareness and conceptual, analytical and operational skills relevant to the social, environmental and economic dimensions of rural development, improve the ability to engage with and influence debates on rural development, and increase the ability to access the rural development literature and community. The course is organized around technical, economic and governance issues.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Pratima Kale","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"402B International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"92084","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6735-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004148/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6735-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6751","title":"INT'L HUMAN RIGHTS LAW","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course introduces students to international human rights law (IHRL). In what sense are internationally-defined human rights \"rights\" and in what sense can the instruments which define them be considered \"law\"? How do we know that a claim is actually a \"human right\"? What are the relations among international, regional and national institutions in establishing and enforcing (or not) IHRL? Does IHRL represent an encroachment on national sovereignty? Is the future of IHRL regional? What enforcement mechanisms can we use, and who can decide upon their use? Finally, what redress is there for human rights violations, and how effective is it? Attendance is required in the first class.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Yasmine Ergas","culpa":2,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"1219 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"62781","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6751-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004149/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6751-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6751","title":"INT'L HUMAN RIGHTS LAW","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course introduces students to international human rights law (IHRL). In what sense are internationally-defined human rights \"rights\" and in what sense can the instruments which define them be considered \"law\"? How do we know that a claim is actually a \"human right\"? What are the relations among international, regional and national institutions in establishing and enforcing (or not) IHRL? Does IHRL represent an encroachment on national sovereignty? Is the future of IHRL regional? What enforcement mechanisms can we use, and who can decide upon their use? Finally, what redress is there for human rights violations, and how effective is it? Attendance is required in the first class.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[4],"start":540,"end":650,"startLabel":"9:00am","endLabel":"10:50am","location":"402 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"80035","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6751-20153-R01","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004150/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6751-20153-R01/"},{"code":"INAF U6758","title":"SHAPING INT'L ECON POLICY:SOUTHEAST ASIA","subtitle":"SHAPING INT'L ECON POLICY","desc":"Mari E. Pangestu is Indonesia's former Minister of Trade and of Tourism and Creative Economy. She currently is Professor of International Economics at the University of Indonesia and serves on the boards of the Centre for Strategic and International Studies, Jakarta, as well as numerous other NGO and private sector entities. This course will explore the economic policies and reforms undertaken at the national, regional and international level that have led to the economic transformation of Southeast Asia in the past few decades. Professor Pangestu will inform the course with both her perspective as an international economist and a leader in national and international policy-making.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Mari E Pangestu","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"823 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"61537","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6758-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004151/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6758-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6765","title":"EU,US & INTL HUMAN RIGHTS","subtitle":"EU, US & INT'L HUMAN RIGHTS","desc":"Both the European Union and the United States consider the protection of human rights as one of their founding values, and both emphasize the centrality of human rights in their internal and external policies. Yet, despite their joint commitment to human rights and a seemingly large potential for common transatlantic approaches to human rights issues in external relations, the European Union and the United States have diverged considerably in their positions on the conceptualization and promotion of human rights. In this course we will study the European Union's and the United States' respective approaches towards international human rights from a comparative perspective, exploring similarities and differences, as well as the potential for stronger transatlantic cooperation. We will first familiarize ourselves with the legal, institutional and policy background of both actors and with th","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Jan Wouters","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[6,7],"start":600,"end":960,"startLabel":"10:00am","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"324 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":1.5,"pointsLabel":"1.5","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"82099","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6765-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004152/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6765-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6796","title":"WAR & CAPTIVITY","subtitle":null,"desc":"As long as societies have gone to war, commanders have had to consider how they will treat captives. It can be a factor at every stage of a struggle, from negotiations to avert war, tactics and strategy for winning, and post-conflict resolution. And long after the end of fighting, the experience of captivity can continue to shape how people recall and commemorate their history. This course examines how generations of lawmakers, diplomats, military commanders and activists have dealt with the problem of captivity. It will also explore the experience of the captives themselves, as well as their guards, including those guards who themselves were made prisoner after being accused of war crimes. Students will become familiar not just with different kinds of modern conflict, but also the different disciplinary methods for studying it, from sociology and political science to philosophy and inte","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Sarah C Kovner","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":660,"end":770,"startLabel":"11:00am","endLabel":"12:50pm","location":"409 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"60036","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6796-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004153/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6796-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6798","title":"CENT ISSUES-AM FOREIGN POLICY","subtitle":null,"desc":"This course examines the sources, substance, and enduring themes of American foreign policy.  Part I reviews the rise of American power in world affairs from the 18th Century through the end of the Cold War.  Part II provides an overview of the process and politics of American foreign policy making.  Part III applies the theory and history of Part I, and the process of Part II, to examine a number of contemporary U.S. foreign policy issues and debates, including America's two wars with Iraq; America's responses to the threat of global terrorism and the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction; and what role the U.S. should play in the world economy, global and regional institutions, and the developing world.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Stuart M Gottlieb","culpa":12,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":850,"end":960,"startLabel":"2:10pm","endLabel":"4:00pm","location":"407 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"11798","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6798-20153-001","openTo":["Architecture","Engineering and Applied Science: Graduate","Global Programs","Graduate School of Arts and Science","International and Public Affairs","Planning","Public Health","School of Professional Studies","Social Work","and Preservation"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004154/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6798-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6800","title":"CONCEPTUAL FOUND OF INTL POLIT","subtitle":null,"desc":"Introduces central concepts and approaches from a variety of social science perspectives, particularly comparative politics and international relations, used to explain, analyze, and evaluate international politics and economics. Designed to help students think theoretically and analytically about leading issues in international affairs by introducing them to social science methods and scholarship and by exposing them to the uses of such concepts in practice, through examination of contemporary problems and challenges in international affairs.","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Stephen R Sestanovich","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Sestanovich","days":[1],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"309 Havemeyer Hall","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":0,"pointsLabel":"0","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"95847","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6800-20153-001","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"LECTURE","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004155/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6800-20153-001/"},{"code":"INAF U6804","title":"CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS-DISC","subtitle":null,"desc":"Through a review of major academic writings, lectures, and class discussion, Conceptual Foundations of International Politics examines many of the central concepts, theories, and analytical tools used in contemporary social science to understand and explain international affairs.  The theoretical literature is drawn from different fields in the social sciences, including comparative politics, international relations, political sociology and economics; the lecturers include members of the Columbia faculty who are authorities in these fields (as well as, in many cases, experienced practitioners in their own right).  The course is designed to enhance students' abilities to think critically and analytically about current problems and challenges in international politics.Conceptual Foundations is a semester-long course.  The lecture/plenary session meets on Monday, and the seminar-style secti","dept":"International and Public Affairs","deptCode":"INAF","instructor":"Bjorn W Gomes","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[3],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"418 International Affairs Building","campus":"Morningside","method":null,"points":4,"pointsLabel":"4","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"10539","enrolled":null,"capacity":null,"enrollSeries":null,"enrollLastDate":null,"classId":"U6804-20153-011","openTo":["International and Public Affairs"],"type":"DISCUSSION","link":"https://web.archive.org///web/20160212004206/http://www.columbia.edu/cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/INAF/U6804-20153-011/"},{"code":"INAF U6804","title":"CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS-DISC","subtitle":null,"desc":"Through a review of major academic writings, lectures, and class discussion, Conceptual Foundations of International Politics examines many of the central concepts, theories, and analytical tools used in contemp